## UI → Service Wiring Rules

### General
- UI components and screens MUST NOT import services directly.
- UI components MUST NOT import `container.ts`.
- UI components MUST interact with business logic ONLY via hooks.
### Composition Hooks
- Screens may use composition hooks that orchestrate multiple services.
- Composition hooks MUST live in `src/hooks`.
- Composition hooks MUST NOT contain business rules.
- Composition hooks are allowed to call multiple services in parallel.
- Composition hooks MUST NOT create new domain services.

### Hooks
- Hooks are the ONLY layer allowed to import services from `src/container.ts`.
- Hooks may compose multiple service calls but MUST NOT contain business rules.
- Hooks that import services MUST live in `src/hooks`.
- Feature hooks map to a single domain capability.
- Screen hooks may compose multiple feature hooks or services.
- Screen hooks MUST NOT contain business logic.
- UI screens MUST NOT orchestrate services directly.
- Screen hooks must be screen-specific and not reused arbitrarily.
- Hooks must not use inheritance or base hooks.
- Shared hook behavior must be extracted into reusable helper hooks or plain utilities.
- Feature hooks should remain thin and explicit.

### Mapping Convention
- Each screen MUST use one or more feature hooks.

### Naming Conventions
- Hook name MUST reflect the service intent:
  - `useNetWorth` → `netWorthService`
  - `useXirrRefresh` → `xirrRefreshService`
  - `useCategoryHierarchy` → `categoryHierarchyService`

### Examples
- Portfolio / Dashboard screens → NetWorthService
- Refresh / Background jobs → SnapshotRefreshService, XirrRefreshService
- Semantic mapping → TransactionSemanticService
- Category trees → CategoryHierarchyService

### Forbidden
- UI MUST NOT call service methods inline.
- UI MUST NOT perform data transformations.
- UI MUST NOT orchestrate multiple services directly.
